A: I am not able to execute C# code as I don't have access to .NET environment. But as an alternative, for unit testing any object method, you can use mocking frameworks like Moq or NSubstitute, which allow you to verify if a method was called and how many times, mock the return of a method, etc. For instance: 

```csharp
// Arrange
var mockList = new Mock<IList<string>>();
mockList.Setup(m => m.Add("foo")).Callback<string>(paramName => 
{
   // Your Code 
});

// Act
AddMethodUnderTest(mockList.Object);

// Assert
mockList.Verify(m => m.Add("foo"), Times.Once);
```

But for your case, as you are talking about ArrayList, here is how you might test its methods(such as `Add`, `IndexOf`, `Remove` etc.) using NUnit.

```csharp
[Test]
public void TestArrayListMethods()
{
    ArrayList arrList = new ArrayList();

    // Adding items 
    arrList.Add("item1");
    arrList.Add("item2");

    // Checking the count after adding 2 items
    Assert.That(arrList.Count, Is.EqualTo(2));

    // IndexOf
    Assert.That(arrList.IndexOf("item1"), Is.EqualTo(0));

    // Remove
    arrList.Remove("item1");
    Assert.That(arrList.Count, Is.EqualTo(1));

    // Contains
    Assert.That(arrList.Contains("item2"), Is.True);
}
```

Please note that you may need to adjust this code according to the methods you want to test and the type of ArrayList.

